# factory-reach-endpoints.env — the endpoints ./verify-factory-reach.sh probes
# from inside a `factory`-namespace pod (livespec-dev-tooling-sszvvw, narrowed
# to one endpoint by livespec-dev-tooling-5iep7h; livespec plan
# factory-worker-nodes-gitops, epic livespec-sqvj7p, plan assertion 7 as amended
# by freeze ruling R78 of 2026-10-16).
#
# FORMAT: one `NAME=HOST:PORT` per line; `#` comments and blank lines ignored.
# NAME is lowercase `[a-z0-9-]` and is what the check reports when a probe
# fails, so it is the operator-facing identity of the endpoint. The renderer
# refuses a malformed line rather than rendering a Job that probes nonsense.
#
# WHY A COMMITTED FILE RATHER THAN LITERALS IN THE SCRIPT. The work-item
# requires the endpoints to be arguments or committed configuration and never
# memorised. A literal in the script body would make the probe set invisible to
# the operator reading `--dry-run`, and an operator probing a different endpoint
# would have to EDIT the check — which is how a check comes to prove something
# other than what it says. Override one entry with
# `--endpoint NAME=HOST:PORT`, or replace this file with `--endpoints FILE`.
# Through the converge, the same override is
# `-e '{"factory_reach_check_endpoints": ["NAME=HOST:PORT"]}'` on the run-now
# apply, which ../../../../ansible/roles/factory_reach_check forwards as those
# very `--endpoint` arguments and validates before one Job is submitted.
#
# THE ENTRY BELOW IS A TAILNET ADDRESS, AND ./factory-namespace.yaml FORCES
# THAT. Its `factory-egress` NetworkPolicy allows exactly three egress classes:
# cluster DNS, the whole Tailscale CGNAT range `100.64.0.0/10` at any port, and
# TCP 443 off-site with every private range EXCEPTED. So an endpoint named by a
# homelab LAN address (192.168.1.0/24), by a node's own cni0 gateway
# (10.42.0.0/16, which is what a pod's default route resolves to) or by a
# cluster Service address is DENIED to a factory pod by construction, however
# reachable it is from the host. ./DERIVATION.md "The tailnet" derives that rule
# from this endpoint and from the beads Dolt tenant that used to sit beside it.
#
# NO BEADS DOLT TENANT ENDPOINT IS NAMED HERE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE RULING
# RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. Freeze ruling R78 (2026-10-16,
# livespec-dev-tooling-dfixhe) amended plan assertion 7: a factory pod must
# reach the telemetry receiver and pull the sandbox image, and LEDGER ACCESS
# STAYS HOST-SIDE WITH THE DISPATCHER. The dispatcher reaches this repository's
# per-repo tenant on the shared family dolt-server through the
# `with-livespec-env.sh` credential wrapper, whose 1Password-projected
# `BEADS_DOLT_PASSWORD` never enters a sandbox — so a pod granted a route to
# `dolt.server-port` could not authenticate anyway, and granting it would only
# move a secret toward a boundary it is not meant to cross. ./DERIVATION.md "The
# tailnet" records the 2026-10-13 reads behind that: TCP to `127.0.0.1:3307`,
# `172.17.0.1:3307` AND `100.89.189.118:3307` was all REFUSED from inside a live
# hp Docker sandbox, because the server binds loopback on whichever tailnet
# member hosts it.
#
# WHAT A PROBE FOR IT COST, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT MERELY REDUNDANT. The probe
# is FAIL-FAST: it stops at the first unreachable endpoint and reports nothing
# behind it. The committed run of 2026-10-16 (gate 20261016T191450Z-326561) read
# the tenant first and found every covered node refused at
# `beads-dolt-tenant 100.89.189.118:3307` — while each pod RAN, so the image
# pulled, and the telemetry receiver below was NEVER REACHED on any node. An
# endpoint nothing is meant to reach therefore does not fail on its own; it
# blinds the check to everything after it. Do not add it back: nothing about the
# tenant has changed, and the amendment is on record.
#
# telemetry-receiver — the OTLP receiver a dispatch projects into its sandbox as
# `LIVESPEC_SANDBOX_OTEL_ENDPOINT`, read from inside a live hp Docker sandbox on
# 2026-10-13 as `http://100.89.189.118:35129`, where `POST /v1/traces` answered
# HTTP 200. That is the receiver the hp Docker sandboxes use today, which is the
# one this check was asked to prove a pod can reach.
#
# ITS PORT IS THE DISPATCHER'S EPHEMERAL BOUND PORT, so the value below is a
# RECORD of one dispatch rather than a durable address — the same fact that
# keeps any port literal out of ./factory-namespace.yaml's tailnet egress rule.
# A live run must therefore read the value a CURRENT dispatch projects and pass
# it: `--endpoint telemetry-receiver=HOST:PORT` to this script, or
# `-e '{"factory_reach_check_endpoints": ["telemetry-receiver=HOST:PORT"]}'` on
# the converge's run-now apply. R78 is explicit that an apply against the
# address below proves nothing about a receiver that exists NOW. Left as
# committed it proves the tailnet PATH to the dispatcher host and reports the
# port as unreachable once that dispatcher is gone, which is the honest answer
# for an address that no longer exists.
#
# NOT the CI lane's receiver, and not the host collector's docker-bridge
# listener. ../arc/hook-pod-template.yaml posts a CI runner pod's spans to the
# host collector's keyless `otlp/pods` listener on each node's own cni0 gateway
# at 4319, and `ansible/roles/fabro_server/files/otel.conf` names
# `http://172.17.0.1:4318` for a Docker sandbox beside its dispatcher. Both
# addresses are inside ranges the `factory-egress` policy excepts, so neither is
# an endpoint a factory pod may reach at all.
telemetry-receiver=100.89.189.118:35129
